When you look at others, you by reason ought to be able to see what it is
carrying & wearing.  Which of those orcs is the one that's empty-handed, and
which has the weapon?  Which has no armor?  Is your pet still carrying that 
wand around, or should you go back and check the passageway?  

Therefore I propose that when you use the / key to identify an object, you 
get the message  "Tame dog named Trojan.  Trojan is carrying a blessed lamp."
or "Soldier.  The soldier is wearing a gold ring [if you don't yet recognize
the ring type], a rusty pot and leather armor.  It is wielding a dagger.  More
info?"  You wouldn't, of course, know the bonuses on the armor until trying it
on - though it would be nice.

*light bulb blinks on*

Thought for a new intrinsic:  The ability to distinguish bonuses on armor
WITHOUT trying it on, just as some people can recognize fine wool and others
couldn't tell silk from denim.  Probably conveyed by nymphs and succumbi (since
they love finery) as well, of course, by shopkeepers if you dare eat them.
Better yet:  There should be an increasing probability of getting it (without
eating anything) the more armor you try on, just as in real life the more you
compare clothing, the better you recognize the difference.  The probability of
gaining it would also be related to your intelligence.
	This intrinsic would also apply to rings, since one can learn how to
judge quality in jewelry as well.  Possibly might be gained separately, though
one sees so few rings in a typical game, my vote would be to lump the two
together.

